 "claims": [
  {
   "id": "B_hook",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Andrew C. Thornton II, a former Lexington, Kentucky police narcotics officer, dropped duffel bags of cocaine from a plane over north Georgia in September 1985; a black bear later got into one of the bags. Narration: 'An ex-narcotics cop dropped cocaine.'",
   "sources": [
    "wiki_t",
    "bio",
    "id",
    "wate",
    "adn",
    "aol_hl",
    "wiki_b",
    "fox5"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_packets",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The bear was found beside a torn duffel bag and about 40 ripped-open (empty) packages of cocaine scattered over the hillside; GBI agent Gary Garner said the bear 'tore the duffel bag open'. Narration: 'A bear ripped open 40 packets.'",
   "sources": [
    "mfloss",
    "yahoo",
    "fox5",
    "wsb"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_cop",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Thornton joined the Lexington Police Department as a narcotics officer in 1968 and resigned in 1977; he also earned a law degree. Narration: 'He used to chase drug dealers.'",
   "sources": [
    "aol_hl",
    "wiki_t",
    "id",
    "wate"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_sides",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "He became a drug smuggler; news reports in 1981-82 linked him (and other Lexington officers) to a smuggling ring known as 'The Company'; he had already served prison time in a marijuana-smuggling case. Narration: 'Then he switched sides.'",
   "sources": [
    "wiki_t",
    "aol_hl",
    "crimelib",
    "wate",
    "mil"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_drop",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "In September 1985 he dumped duffel bags of cocaine from the plane over north Georgia. Narration: 'Over Georgia, he dumped the bags.'",
   "sources": [
    "wiki_t",
    "bio",
    "wate",
    "fox5",
    "hnn"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_para",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "FUN FACT CARD: he was a former (Army) paratrooper. Card: 'HE WAS A FORMER ARMY PARATROOPER'. Narration: 'Then he jumped too.' (he jumped from the plane over Knoxville, Tennessee)",
   "sources": [
    "mil",
    "wate",
    "wiki_t",
    "bio"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_fail",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "His parachute failed (opened too late / he became entangled; the GBI said he struck the plane's tail); he was found dead on 11 September 1985 by an elderly resident in a Knoxville yard / gravel driveway. Narration: 'His parachute failed.' Label: 'FOUND DEAD · KNOXVILLE, TENN.'",
   "sources": [
    "wiki_t",
    "oxygen",
    "adn",
    "wate",
    "bio"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_gucci",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "He was found wearing night-vision goggles, a bulletproof vest and Gucci loafers. Narration: 'He wore Gucci loafers ...'; label 'ALSO: NIGHT-VISION GOGGLES, A BULLETPROOF VEST'",
   "sources": [
    "adn",
    "oxygen",
    "hnn",
    "id"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_79",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "STATISTIC (counter): about 79 lb of cocaine was strapped to him / in the bag at his side (the New York Times, Sept 1985: '79 pounds of cocaine at his side'; others round to 'about 75-80 lb'; reported street value about $15 million). Narration: '... and 79 pounds of cocaine.'",
   "sources": [
    "adn",
    "wate",
    "oxygen",
    "bio",
    "mil"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_found",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "About three months later (around 20 December 1985; reported by AP and the New York Times, 23 December 1985) Georgia Bureau of Investigation agents found a dead American black bear (reported 150-175 lb) in Fannin County, north Georgia (Chattahoochee National Forest). Narration: 'Three months later, agents found a dead bear.'",
   "sources": [
    "wiki_b",
    "mfloss",
    "yahoo",
    "fox5",
    "wsb",
    "snopes"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_stomach",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The state's chief medical examiner, Dr Kenneth Alonso, said the bear's stomach was 'literally packed to the brim with cocaine' (and estimated 3-4 g reached its bloodstream; cause of death: acute cocaine intoxication). Narration: 'Its stomach was packed with cocaine.'; card: 'STATE MEDICAL EXAMINER, 1985'",
   "sources": [
    "wiki_b",
    "aol_tox",
    "wsb",
    "snopes"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_plane",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "After he jumped, the pilotless twin-engine plane flew on (on autopilot) and crashed in Hayesville, North Carolina, more than 60 miles away. Narration: 'And his empty plane? It crashed in North Carolina.'",
   "sources": [
    "wiki_t",
    "tbs",
    "looper",
    "bio"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_lesson",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "LESSON CARD (practical, for the viewer): if you find a suspicious package (possible drugs), do not touch, pick up or open it (Matagorda County, Texas, and Indian River County, Florida, sheriff's offices); Matagorda County: once a person picks up the package they are in possession of it and could be charged; note where it is and call law enforcement (Walton County, Florida, sheriff; Matagorda; FBI: on forest land, leave the area and report). Rows: 'DON'T TOUCH OR OPEN IT', 'PICKING IT UP = POSSESSION', 'NOTE THE SPOT, CALL POLICE'.",
   "sources": [
    "khou",
    "nbcdfw",
    "cbstx",
    "vero",
    "walton",
    "fbi_mke"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_stuffed",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "reported",
   "text": "REPORTED: a taxidermied bear said to be the Cocaine Bear, nicknamed 'Pablo EskoBear', is on display at the Kentucky for Kentucky Fun Mall in Lexington, Kentucky; its chain of custody rests mainly on the shop's own account and its authenticity is disputed (WAVE 3; Saving Country Music). Narration: 'Today, its stuffed remains reportedly stand in Lexington.'",
   "sources": [
    "wiki_b",
    "mfloss_mall",
    "syfy",
    "wave",
    "scm",
    "tifo"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_irony",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Lexington is the city whose police department Thornton served as a narcotics officer (1968-77). Narration: 'The same city the cop once policed.'",
   "sources": [
    "aol_hl",
    "wiki_t",
    "id"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B_film",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Card (text only): the 2023 film 'Cocaine Bear' (directed by Elizabeth Banks) is loosely based on the story.",
   "sources": [
    "wrap",
    "bio",
    "wiki_b",
    "mil"
   ]
  }
 ],
